This hands-on exercise demonstrates the practical application of geospatial predictive modelling using the geographical random forest (GRF) method. It builds on the key principle that spatial events are neither randomly nor uniformly distributed, guiding you to explore how historical geospatial occurrences can be linked with environmental variables that reflect spatial constraints and influences.
The workflow involves preparing training and test datasets through appropriate spatial sampling techniques, calibrating predictive models using both geospatial statistical learning and machine learning approaches, and performing model comparison and selection to identify the best predictive model. Finally, the chosen model is used to generate future outcome predictions. Together, these steps illustrate how known spatial patterns and predictor variables can be leveraged to forecast future events within a geospatial framework.


In this hands-on exercise, predictive modelling will be implemented by using the geographical random forest method.
The process involves:

preparing training and test data sets by using appropriate data sampling methods;
calibrating predictive models by using geospatial statistical learning and machine learning methods;
comparing and selecting the best model for predicting the future outcome;
predicting the future outcomes by using the best calibrated model.

